{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Memory now supports short-term memory retention of up to 10 years - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock has updated AgentCore Memory to allow for short-term memory retention of up to 10 years. Previously, this memory only retained data for one year. With this new feature, developers can use short-term memory as a long-term record of agent conversations and interactions. This is beneficial for compliance and audit purposes, as well as for users who return after extended absences or for agents requiring access to past sessions. Retention is set at the memory resource level and applies to each event upon creation, with existing events maintaining their original retention. Standard short-term memory storage charges apply for the duration of data retention. This feature is available in all AWS Regions where AgentCore Memory is supported. To utilize this feature, refer to the AgentCore Memory documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/11/amazon-bedrock-agentcore-memory-10-year-retention/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up or migrating Knowledge Bases to Managed, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"amazon-workspaces-agent-access","skill_description":"Connects AI agents to Windows desktops on Amazon WorkSpaces Applications (AppStream 2.0) fleets and image builders through the Agent Access MCP server, and guides reliable desktop automation. Covers the MCP endpoint and SigV4 signing, naming the session (stack and user ID, streaming URL, or AD SAML/OIDC), connecting to an image builder as one of its Windows accounts (switch_user), BLOCKING vs POLLING connect modes, the computer-use tools and computer use interface header, screenshot and batching discipline, forwarded MCP tools (forwarded___), screenshot uploads and Replay Session recordings in S3, agentaccess-mcp IAM, session lifecycle, and errors like \"dcv session not ready\", \"client_disconnected\" and 400 signing-region. Use when building or debugging an agent that drives a desktop or GUI app on WorkSpaces Applications / AppStream. Not for setting up or configuring desktops, fleets, stacks, image builders or agent access (see setting-up-workspaces-applications), or for Amazon WorkSpaces Personal/Core.\n\nServices: workspaces-applications, appstream, agentaccess-mcp, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: connect, automate, debug, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: desktop-automation, agent-tooling","skill_name":"amazon-workspaces-agent-access"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"writing-agentcore-policy","skill_description":"Authors, validates and submits Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Policy statements (Cedar and Dogwood) for an AgentCore Gateway. Discovers the Gateway's targets and tools, generates the Gateway's Cedar schema, drafts permit/forbid, guardrail and temporal policies from access requirements or from a whole natural-language policy document (splitting it into rules, marking the rules that cannot be expressed, and repairing failing drafts in batches), checks them locally (syntax, schema, API limits, claim and guardrail pitfalls), and creates or updates them in a policy engine after explicit developer approval, reporting the final status and service findings. Use when a developer wants to write, translate, fix, validate or submit AgentCore Gateway policies, convert a policy document or a list of rules into policies, restrict which users or roles can call Gateway tools, add Bedrock guardrail or session-history (temporal) conditions, or resolve CreatePolicy/UpdatePolicy failures.\n\nServices: bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: design, deploy, debug, secure\nPersona: developer, security\nWorkload: agents, generative-ai","skill_name":"writing-agentcore-policy"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Search AWS docs on AgentCore memory behavior"}
